Every person taking photographs for the purposes of the National Registration Act, shall comply with the following conditions —

(a) the paper used for photographs shall be of single weight, semi matt surface, unglazed and of a make approved by the Commissioner;

(b) the minimum thickness of paper shall be 0.076 millimeters and the maximum thickness 0.178 millimeters;

(c) the size of the photograph shall be 35 milimeters by 45 millimeters and the photograph shall be without border. The full face and both ears of the person photographed shall appear and the image from the chin to the top of the head shall be not less than 26 millimeters in a vertical position. The photograph shall be taken against a plain background and balanced lighting shall be used so that no shadows shall be shown;

(d) the photographs shall be finished in colour. No tinting, toning or retouching shall be permitted;

(e) (Revoked);

(f) the photograph of a person should be taken without his headgear but any person who habitually wears a headgear in accordance with his religious or racial custom may be photographed with his headgear provided that such headgear does not hide his features;

(g) (Revoked); p. 4 2002 Ed.] National Registration [CAP. 19, Rg 2

(h) (Revoked).
